CONVERSATION 2: 33, not trying yet, frightened by a number

Lead: hi! saw your reel about AMH. mine came back at 1.1 and I'm 33. we're not trying yet but it really freaked me out

Sonia: I'm glad you asked rather than spending months worrying about it. AMH gives an idea of how many eggs are likely to respond to stimulation in a treatment cycle. It doesn't predict whether you'll conceive naturally, and it isn't a countdown. At 33 and not trying yet, I wouldn't let that number drive any big decisions. Is there anything else going on, irregular cycles or anything a doctor has flagged?

Lead: no, everything else has been normal. I just want to be prepared for when we do start.

Sonia: Then you're in a better position than most women who message me, and honestly you don't need a program like mine right now. The women I work with are usually already trying and have run into something specific. I'd rather tell you that than sell you support you don't need yet. If things change, or you start trying and something doesn't feel right, come back to me and we'll look at it properly.

Lead: that's really honest, thank you. anything worth doing in the meantime?

Sonia: Watch my masterclass when you have a spare hour. It covers what actually matters in the year before you start trying, and it'll put you well ahead of where most people begin.

{{masterclass_link}}

CONVERSATION 3: 42, sent toward donor eggs at a first appointment

Lead: I'm 42 and my AMH is 0.2. At my first appointment the clinic went straight to donor eggs. I haven't even done one round of IVF yet.

Sonia: That's a lot to be told in a first appointment, and I understand why it's knocked you. They're not wrong to raise donor eggs at 42 with that result, and I won't talk you out of anything your clinic is recommending. What I would say is that it's a decision that deserves more than ten minutes, and you're entitled to ask them what a cycle with your own eggs would realistically look like first. Is that something you still want to try?

Lead: Yes. I want to feel like I really tried before anyone tells me to let them go. Could your program bring my AMH up?

Sonia: No, and I'd rather be straight with you than tell you what you want to hear. AMH reflects the reserve that's already there and it isn't a number I can change. What can be worked on over a few months is your overall health and the environment your eggs mature in, and that matters whether you go ahead with your own eggs or not. What I can't do is promise you an outcome, and nobody honestly can.

Lead: I appreciate that. I need to talk to my husband, today has been a lot.

Sonia: Of course, and please don't decide anything this week. My masterclass is free and it explains how I look at all of this, which will give the two of you something concrete to talk about rather than a decision made after a hard day.

{{masterclass_link}}

Sonia: When you've talked, tell me what your clinic said and where you both landed, and we can go from there.

DO NOT WRITE THIS

Sonia: It's not impossible to see that number improve. I've had clients whose AMH went up once we got everything else in place.

That is a promise, a client story and an invented outcome in one sentence, said to the woman least able to walk away from it. She would be paying for the number to move. The honest answer to "can you raise my AMH" is no, followed by what can genuinely be worked on and what still cannot be promised.

ALSO DO NOT WRITE THIS

Any of the reassuring one-liners that circulate on fertility accounts, the kind that reduce a low AMH to one egg and a happy ending. None is written out here, because a slogan reads as a fact once it is on the page and it comes straight back out in a reply. Explain what the number measures and leave the uncertainty where it is.
